PICONIX FINITE COMMAND REFERENCE FOR AGENTS

READ-ONLY, USUALLY FINITE
  help [CMD]             command syntax or index
  ps                     process table
  lsof [PID]             FD table
  ls [-la] [PATH]        directory entries
  tree [-L 0..8] [PATH]  tree, depth limit
  find [PATH] [-name P]  entry lookup
  cat [-n] [FILE]        finite files only
  grep PATTERN [FILE]    literal, case-sensitive, no -i
  hexdump [F [O [N]]]   bounded when count supplied
  ip [addr]              IPv4 interface snapshot
  net [-a|-r]            Wi-Fi status, clients, ARP
  pins                   pin snapshot
  pwmctl status          PWM snapshot
  trace show [PID]       stops recording
  cat /proc/memory       kernel memory summary

SIDE EFFECTS / POTENTIAL BLOCKING
  cc /tmp/foo.c          writes generated .s
  as /tmp/foo.s          overwrites /tmp/a.out
  python /tmp/foo.py     executes user program
  rm FILE                deletes RAM file
  cp SRC DST             overwrites destination
  dd ...                 binary read/write; check flags
  pwmctl freq HZ         affects both PWM channels
  i2cctl scan            issues actual I2C read probes
  i2cctl write           changes sensor/device registers
  kill PID               ends a task
  trace on/off           changes trace capture mode

INTERACTIVE / LONG-RUNNING
  fd, edit, vi, mem, gpio, fbed, watch, pong, reversi, mb,
  trace follow, bare python, indefinite tcpdump and listeners.
Do not launch these via MCP execute_command.
For Reversi keys, turns and PTY usage, read /man/reversi.

SHELL LIMITS
  Supports simple pipelines and redirections, not full bash.
  No assumptions about GNU flags, POSIX job control, globbing,
  command substitution, environment expansion or shell scripts.

SHELL: see shell-syntax for exact operators in interactive sh and
MCP sh -c. rm accepts exactly one path, not GNU rm a b c.
